1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:29,980
We'll be right back.

2
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:59,980
We'll be right back.

3
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:03,900
Welcome back to our series on the parables.

4
00:01:03,900 --> 00:01:08,540
This is week four, so if this is your first time joining us, you've missed a little bit,

5
00:01:08,540 --> 00:01:09,840
but we can catch you up pretty quickly.

6
00:01:09,840 --> 00:01:10,660
Shouldn't be a problem.

7
00:01:10,660 --> 00:01:13,040
Hopefully, you've been following along with us.

8
00:01:13,040 --> 00:01:18,780
We have talked about how one-third of the red letters in the gospel, one-third of the

9
00:01:18,780 --> 00:01:21,880
words that Jesus speaks in the Gospels are spoken in parables.

10
00:01:22,000 --> 00:01:29,680
These are very important lessons, stories that Jesus gives to us, and they're also very

11
00:01:29,680 --> 00:01:32,120
easy to misunderstand or misapply.

12
00:01:32,120 --> 00:01:37,500
It's easy to read things into them that aren't there or to miss things that are there because

13
00:01:37,500 --> 00:01:39,540
we're 2,000 years removed from a culture.

14
00:01:40,200 --> 00:01:43,640
And so, although we don't have time to cover all the parables, my hope is that we give

15
00:01:43,640 --> 00:01:48,120
you some tools in your toolbox so that as you read and come across parables in your own Scripture

16
00:01:48,120 --> 00:01:51,440
reading, because all of you read the Scriptures on a regular basis by yourselves.

17
00:01:51,440 --> 00:01:53,920
Thank you.

18
00:01:53,920 --> 00:01:59,500
So that as you come across these parables, you can hopefully have some tools in your toolbox

19
00:01:59,500 --> 00:02:01,080
to understand them for yourselves.

20
00:02:01,080 --> 00:02:05,520
As we said at the beginning, that parables are indirect communication aimed at challenging

21
00:02:05,520 --> 00:02:07,360
your previously held assumptions.

22
00:02:07,360 --> 00:02:11,740
All of us love to have our previously held assumptions challenged, correct?

23
00:02:11,740 --> 00:02:14,720
Jesus does them creatively.

24
00:02:14,720 --> 00:02:16,240
He goes in through the back door.

25
00:02:16,240 --> 00:02:19,260
He appeals to our imagination, to our emotions.

26
00:02:19,260 --> 00:02:21,680
He doesn't just come right out and say, you're wrong.

27
00:02:21,680 --> 00:02:27,000
He tells you a story that if you take it to heart and walk away with it and think about

28
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:29,400
it, you'll realize that he was telling you that you were wrong.

29
00:02:30,340 --> 00:02:35,200
And maybe you'll be able to hear it rather than just confronting you directly.

30
00:02:35,200 --> 00:02:40,160
In context, they're directly confronting the nation of Israel's ideas in the first century.

31
00:02:40,160 --> 00:02:43,520
He is speaking to a specific audience for a specific reason.

32
00:02:43,520 --> 00:02:48,020
And so in order for us to understand how they apply to our lives, to interpret them correctly,

33
00:02:48,020 --> 00:02:51,100
we first have to understand the intended meaning.

34
00:02:51,100 --> 00:02:54,060
And then we can get some significance for ourselves.

35
00:02:54,060 --> 00:02:57,740
And last week, I introduced these three categories.

36
00:02:58,260 --> 00:03:02,600
I said that scholars have been trying to categorize parables in many different ways for a very

37
00:03:02,600 --> 00:03:03,200
long time.

38
00:03:03,200 --> 00:03:06,500
And you could find a plethora of categories.

39
00:03:06,500 --> 00:03:12,160
But because we are a church of real people with real lives, I chose a category of only three

40
00:03:12,160 --> 00:03:13,880
so that we would remember them.

41
00:03:13,880 --> 00:03:18,700
The three categories we talked about are the surprising arrival of the kingdom.

42
00:03:18,700 --> 00:03:24,920
So Jesus talks, tells parables in such a way that he talks about the way the kingdom comes in

43
00:03:24,920 --> 00:03:26,120
unexpected ways.

44
00:03:26,120 --> 00:03:31,920
The first century Jews, a lot of them had very concrete ideas about how God's kingdom was

45
00:03:31,920 --> 00:03:32,640
going to come.

46
00:03:32,640 --> 00:03:35,640
And the reality of God's kingdom was different than what they were expecting.

47
00:03:36,200 --> 00:03:41,140
And so Jesus would tell these parables to try and help them broaden their horizons about what

48
00:03:41,140 --> 00:03:43,600
the arrival of the kingdom might look like.

49
00:03:43,600 --> 00:03:48,040
Second category is the surprising ethic of the kingdom of God.

50
00:03:48,040 --> 00:03:53,500
Again, there were previously held assumptions, things about who the kingdom was for, about how

51
00:03:53,500 --> 00:03:54,620
the kingdom would work.

52
00:03:54,620 --> 00:03:59,200
And Jesus tells these parables, one that we're going to cover today about the surprising ethic

53
00:03:59,200 --> 00:04:04,700
of God's kingdom, the invitation to obey it radically.

54
00:04:04,700 --> 00:04:11,020
And the last is as he gets closer to Jerusalem, he gets more and more clear about what he's

55
00:04:11,020 --> 00:04:11,440
saying.

56
00:04:11,440 --> 00:04:13,480
And he has these crisis of choice parables.

57
00:04:13,480 --> 00:04:15,100
The kingdom is coming.

58
00:04:15,100 --> 00:04:16,780
The ethic is surprising.

59
00:04:16,780 --> 00:04:18,860
What will you do about it as the listener?

60
00:04:18,860 --> 00:04:21,120
Will you accept it or will you reject it?

61
00:04:21,120 --> 00:04:25,200
And he talks about the consequences of what happens if you accept it and if you reject

62
00:04:25,200 --> 00:04:25,320
it.

63
00:04:25,320 --> 00:04:26,860
We're going to get to those in a couple of weeks.

64
00:04:26,860 --> 00:04:28,460
They should be very fun.

65
00:04:28,460 --> 00:04:30,240
But for now, that's the context.

66
00:04:30,240 --> 00:04:30,960
That's where we've been.

67
00:04:30,960 --> 00:04:33,320
Does this sound familiar if you've been here for a couple of weeks?

68
00:04:33,320 --> 00:04:34,380
Good.

69
00:04:34,380 --> 00:04:34,860
All right.

70
00:04:34,860 --> 00:04:35,460
Thank goodness.

71
00:04:35,460 --> 00:04:35,800
All right.

72
00:04:35,800 --> 00:04:37,000
Let's pray and get started.

73
00:04:37,000 --> 00:04:39,800
Oh, Abba in heaven.

74
00:04:39,800 --> 00:04:48,220
We are so thankful for yet another week with breath in our lungs and an opportunity to sit

75
00:04:48,220 --> 00:04:50,700
here in a group of people that are considering your teachings.

76
00:04:51,520 --> 00:04:54,040
That are open to what you have to say to us.

77
00:04:54,040 --> 00:04:55,960
God, we all come in here with different baggage.

78
00:04:55,960 --> 00:05:01,000
We all come in here with different previously held ideas about you and what you're like and

79
00:05:01,000 --> 00:05:02,180
how you interact with us.

80
00:05:02,180 --> 00:05:08,660
I pray this morning that you would soften our hearts and that we would be able to interact

81
00:05:08,660 --> 00:05:13,620
with your teaching in a way that allows us to be confronted with the truth.

82
00:05:14,940 --> 00:05:20,740
Convicted, challenged, encouraged, and leave this place with a better idea of who you are,

83
00:05:20,740 --> 00:05:23,160
of what you're like, and of what you're inviting us into.

84
00:05:23,160 --> 00:05:27,520
God, do that for your glory and for our good and for the good of our neighbor.

85
00:05:28,340 --> 00:05:29,900
It's in Jesus' name I pray.

86
00:05:29,900 --> 00:05:30,760
Amen.

87
00:05:30,760 --> 00:05:38,640
I don't know if you've ever had the opportunity to ask a question of someone that you looked

88
00:05:38,640 --> 00:05:38,980
up to.

89
00:05:38,980 --> 00:05:40,320
Maybe it was in a college class.

90
00:05:40,320 --> 00:05:41,600
Maybe it was at a Q&A.

91
00:05:42,260 --> 00:05:47,900
You had this opportunity to ask somebody that you look up to a question, or maybe you had

92
00:05:47,900 --> 00:05:52,780
the opportunity to ask a question of someone that is in a position of authority or power

93
00:05:52,780 --> 00:05:57,720
that you totally disagree with, and you thought you were going to ask the question that was

94
00:05:57,720 --> 00:06:01,300
just going to expose their hypocrisy or their bad ideology.

95
00:06:01,300 --> 00:06:02,780
I don't know if you've ever been in this situation.

96
00:06:02,780 --> 00:06:06,560
Maybe you showed up to a town hall to talk to a local politician.

97
00:06:06,560 --> 00:06:08,300
I don't know if you've ever been in this situation.

98
00:06:08,300 --> 00:06:14,020
I've been in situations like this previously, and if you're anything like me, I tend to

99
00:06:14,020 --> 00:06:16,520
psychoanalyze conversations that haven't yet happened.

100
00:06:16,520 --> 00:06:23,060
And so I remember these opportunities to ask questions of people either I looked up to or

101
00:06:23,060 --> 00:06:28,000
I was trying to confront, and I would work so hard on wordsmithing, on crafting the perfect

102
00:06:28,000 --> 00:06:31,000
question, and I had two motivations lying behind that.

103
00:06:31,000 --> 00:06:36,960
The first motivation was if I'm asking a question of somebody I look up to, I wanted to word a

104
00:06:36,960 --> 00:06:40,680
question in such a way that I would impress the person I was asking a question of.

105
00:06:40,680 --> 00:06:44,220
You know, they get questions asked all the time, but I wanted to be the one that asked

106
00:06:44,220 --> 00:06:47,120
the question, and they were like, wow, this guy gets it.

107
00:06:47,120 --> 00:06:49,720
You know, what a great question.

108
00:06:49,720 --> 00:06:58,820
Or I worked so hard on crafting a question to expose the false belief of somebody that I disagreed

109
00:06:58,820 --> 00:06:59,060
with.

110
00:06:59,060 --> 00:07:03,500
So again, I don't know if any of you have had these experiences before, but I know for me,

111
00:07:03,500 --> 00:07:08,440
when I've had these opportunities, they often do not go the way that I think they will.

112
00:07:08,440 --> 00:07:11,800
I often don't get the response that I thought I would.

113
00:07:11,800 --> 00:07:18,520
Maybe I'm not as impressive as I think I am, or maybe the person I disagree with isn't as

114
00:07:18,520 --> 00:07:19,980
wrong as I thought they were.

115
00:07:19,980 --> 00:07:26,200
But this is kind of the setting, the context for the parable that we're coming into today.

116
00:07:26,300 --> 00:07:31,260
There is a expert in the Old Testament law, an expert in the Jewish Bible, and he has this

117
00:07:31,260 --> 00:07:33,720
opportunity to come to Jesus with a question.

118
00:07:33,720 --> 00:07:38,440
And we don't fully know what his motivations are, but we know that he has this one opportunity

119
00:07:38,440 --> 00:07:44,340
that he's worked really hard on this question, and that he's going to get an answer that he

120
00:07:44,340 --> 00:07:45,940
does not expect.

121
00:07:46,720 --> 00:07:50,400
It comes in the form of a parable that's very familiar to most of us.

122
00:07:50,400 --> 00:07:57,060
And what I want to do from the outset is I want to caution us to read this parable slowly,

123
00:07:57,060 --> 00:07:59,640
to wrestle with its implications.

124
00:07:59,640 --> 00:08:06,320
I think we have a tendency to read the Scriptures, especially the Scriptures that are familiar to

125
00:08:06,320 --> 00:08:06,460
us.

126
00:08:06,460 --> 00:08:11,320
We read them quickly without nuance in a way that allows us to demonize the perceived enemies

127
00:08:11,320 --> 00:08:13,340
in the text, right?

128
00:08:13,340 --> 00:08:15,820
It's like, ooh, that guy messed up.

129
00:08:15,820 --> 00:08:21,560
And so we read these texts and we think, okay, I'm bad, but I'm not as bad as that guy.

130
00:08:21,560 --> 00:08:27,480
We have a way of demonizing the characters in the story, and we're not able to see the implications

131
00:08:27,480 --> 00:08:30,680
that that might have on our lives.

132
00:08:30,680 --> 00:08:32,800
I think we've done this with the parable of the Good Samaritan.

133
00:08:32,800 --> 00:08:37,360
We've demonized certain characters, and we're not able to see the way in which we play the

134
00:08:37,360 --> 00:08:40,260
part of those characters on a regular basis.

135
00:08:41,320 --> 00:08:45,740
And so we're going to get an answer to this question that's unexpected, and it's going

136
00:08:45,740 --> 00:08:51,860
to challenge us to the core, I think, as it did the original audience.

137
00:08:51,860 --> 00:08:54,600
So let's read it, see what God might have for us.

138
00:08:54,600 --> 00:08:57,900
Luke chapter 10, I'll give you a little bit of time.

139
00:08:57,900 --> 00:08:59,860
I usually like to give you a heads up before I get there.

140
00:08:59,860 --> 00:09:02,900
So Luke 10, if you have, I hear some paper flipping.

141
00:09:02,900 --> 00:09:04,880
I'm so thankful for people with paper Bibles.

142
00:09:05,860 --> 00:09:11,560
Those of you with digital Bibles, you're great too, but, you know, it's good to hold it in

143
00:09:11,560 --> 00:09:12,300
your hands sometimes.

144
00:09:12,300 --> 00:09:13,860
I'm old.

145
00:09:13,860 --> 00:09:14,900
I am old.

146
00:09:14,900 --> 00:09:15,520
All right.

147
00:09:15,520 --> 00:09:19,120
Luke chapter 10, verse 25.

148
00:09:19,120 --> 00:09:24,200
On one occasion, an expert in the law stood up to test Jesus.

149
00:09:24,200 --> 00:09:29,400
Teacher, he asked, what must I do to inherit eternal life?

150
00:09:30,740 --> 00:09:34,100
Classic Jesus fashion, responds to a question with a question.

151
00:09:34,100 --> 00:09:35,860
What is written in the law?

152
00:09:35,860 --> 00:09:36,640
Jesus replied.

153
00:09:36,640 --> 00:09:37,720
How do you read it?

154
00:09:37,720 --> 00:09:42,320
The expert in the law answered, love the Lord your God with all of your heart, soul,

155
00:09:42,320 --> 00:09:47,800
with all of your strength, and with all of your mind, and love your neighbor as yourself.

156
00:09:47,800 --> 00:09:51,000
You have answered correctly, Jesus replied.

157
00:09:51,000 --> 00:09:52,840
Do this and you will live.

158
00:09:52,840 --> 00:09:55,660
But he wanted to justify himself.

159
00:09:55,660 --> 00:09:58,360
I'm going to read that again.

160
00:09:58,360 --> 00:10:00,400
He wanted to justify himself.

161
00:10:00,400 --> 00:10:05,300
So he asked Jesus, and who is my neighbor?

162
00:10:05,300 --> 00:10:11,020
Again, an expert in the Jewish Bible gets his one shot, his one opportunity.

163
00:10:11,020 --> 00:10:16,460
Thank you.

164
00:10:16,460 --> 00:10:19,680
The song lyrics worked last week.

165
00:10:19,680 --> 00:10:21,220
I thought I'd try them again one more time.

166
00:10:21,220 --> 00:10:27,180
His one opportunity to ask Jesus this very important, pressing cultural and theological question,

167
00:10:27,180 --> 00:10:30,760
one that we've probably all wrestled with at one point in our lives.

168
00:10:30,760 --> 00:10:33,540
How do we inherit eternal life?

169
00:10:33,540 --> 00:10:38,240
There has to be more than just this life, right?

170
00:10:38,240 --> 00:10:40,520
All of us have wrestled with this question.

171
00:10:40,520 --> 00:10:44,720
We've gone through the difficulties of life, and we've thought there has to be something more.

172
00:10:44,720 --> 00:10:49,900
How do I get this thing that seems like it's so there and real?

173
00:10:50,160 --> 00:10:53,020
How do I inherit eternal life?

174
00:10:53,020 --> 00:10:57,020
The text says this lawyer, this expert, does it to test Jesus.

175
00:10:57,020 --> 00:11:01,040
Now, we don't know exactly what the motivation is.

176
00:11:01,040 --> 00:11:04,040
We don't know if he thinks Jesus is a heretic that he's trying to expose.

177
00:11:04,040 --> 00:11:04,880
He's the expert.

178
00:11:04,880 --> 00:11:09,880
Jesus has got it wrong, and he's going to ask this pointed question and expose Jesus' false theology.

179
00:11:09,880 --> 00:11:13,260
Or maybe he's genuinely intrigued with the ministry of Jesus.

180
00:11:13,260 --> 00:11:14,300
He's seen the miracles.

181
00:11:14,500 --> 00:11:18,760
He's heard testimonies of all of the incredible things Jesus has done.

182
00:11:18,760 --> 00:11:21,440
But he's got some concerns about his theology.

183
00:11:21,440 --> 00:11:27,400
Whatever the motivation is, he wants to know how Jesus would answer this very crucial question.

184
00:11:27,400 --> 00:11:31,580
He wants to know what Jesus thinks about how to inherit eternal life.

185
00:11:32,360 --> 00:11:40,080
Now, eternal life in this Jewish concept is different than we often think about eternal life in our day.

186
00:11:40,080 --> 00:11:44,380
The Jews in this day had a different idea about eternal life than we do today.

187
00:11:44,380 --> 00:11:52,380
The man wasn't primarily asking about an afterlife destination, which is often what we think about when we talk about eternal life.

188
00:11:52,380 --> 00:11:55,940
We hear evangelists preach to us and say, where will you go when you die?

189
00:11:55,940 --> 00:11:57,900
Do you know for certain you'll go to heaven when you die?

190
00:11:57,900 --> 00:12:01,980
This isn't primarily the thought in Jewish time at this day.

191
00:12:01,980 --> 00:12:12,600
They're thinking primarily about a quality of life that can start now and will last through eternity when God comes back and rules and reigns over the world.

192
00:12:12,600 --> 00:12:17,440
When God returns in Jewish thought, all unrighteous ways of living will be destroyed,

193
00:12:17,440 --> 00:12:22,620
and all righteous ways of living will receive eternal life.

194
00:12:23,200 --> 00:12:25,720
So he's asking Jesus, what can I do?

195
00:12:25,720 --> 00:12:29,780
How do I start living now that will last for eternity?

196
00:12:29,780 --> 00:12:34,580
And again, in classic Jesus fashion, he responds to a question with a question.

197
00:12:34,580 --> 00:12:41,000
Now, before we move on, I just want to tell us that this is a good way.

198
00:12:41,000 --> 00:12:44,560
Like, we could learn a lesson from Jesus responding to questions with questions.

199
00:12:45,460 --> 00:12:52,240
And when you interact with people that disagree with you about theology, when you interact with people that disagree with you about God in general,

200
00:12:52,240 --> 00:12:58,460
I think the tendency is to try and come up with an answer to the question that proves that we've thought it through,

201
00:12:58,460 --> 00:13:04,220
that we have a concrete belief around what we're trying to communicate to them.

202
00:13:05,240 --> 00:13:10,880
And again, I just think this is a total side note, but I think as we interact with people that disagree with us,

203
00:13:10,880 --> 00:13:16,060
we would do well to ask them clarifying questions, to help understand where they're coming from.

204
00:13:16,060 --> 00:13:17,620
And so Jesus does this.

205
00:13:17,620 --> 00:13:21,220
He asks this clarifying question, and the man's response is a home run.

206
00:13:21,220 --> 00:13:28,000
He literally answers the same way that Jesus answers in other parts of the gospel.

207
00:13:28,000 --> 00:13:32,060
He says, love the Lord your God with all of your heart, soul, mind, and strength, and love your neighbor as yourself.

208
00:13:32,060 --> 00:13:34,400
He summarized the whole point of the law.

209
00:13:34,400 --> 00:13:38,020
This has been at the foundation of the Jewish faith.

210
00:13:38,020 --> 00:13:41,260
These two commands are the foundation of Jewish faith.

211
00:13:41,260 --> 00:13:42,800
And Jesus says, you nailed it.

212
00:13:42,800 --> 00:13:44,840
You're in good shape.

213
00:13:44,840 --> 00:13:47,260
Do this and live.

214
00:13:47,260 --> 00:13:50,700
That could have been the end, right?

215
00:13:50,700 --> 00:13:51,600
That's it.

216
00:13:51,600 --> 00:13:52,980
He got his question answered.

217
00:13:52,980 --> 00:13:53,980
He got the right answer.

218
00:13:53,980 --> 00:13:58,180
But it says the lawyer just couldn't help himself.

219
00:13:58,180 --> 00:14:01,640
And I don't blame him.

220
00:14:02,060 --> 00:14:09,000
And I don't think we should jump to condemnation from him too quickly, because I think we do this a lot, more often than we might think.

221
00:14:09,000 --> 00:14:10,660
We try and justify ourselves.

222
00:14:10,660 --> 00:14:14,960
We have all of this good theology in the back of our brain.

223
00:14:14,960 --> 00:14:20,020
We want to obey Jesus, but we think, love God with all of your heart, soul, mind, and strength.

224
00:14:20,020 --> 00:14:21,020
Love your neighbor as yourself.

225
00:14:21,020 --> 00:14:22,040
That's not possible.

226
00:14:23,520 --> 00:14:25,580
That's not a possible thing to accomplish.

227
00:14:25,580 --> 00:14:30,240
No matter how great you are at it, you will not perfectly love God with your heart, soul, mind, and strength.

228
00:14:30,240 --> 00:14:32,280
And you will not love your neighbor as yourself.

229
00:14:32,280 --> 00:14:38,300
And so this lawyer is thinking, okay, I get that that's what we're invited to do, but none of us are doing that.

230
00:14:39,300 --> 00:14:49,520
So seeking to justify himself, seeking to use theology to make him be able to categorize himself as someone that's going to have eternal life.

231
00:14:50,660 --> 00:14:54,100
He says, who really is my neighbor?

232
00:14:54,100 --> 00:14:56,480
Because it can't be everybody.

233
00:14:56,480 --> 00:15:03,360
Everybody can't be my neighbor, or I will never be able to live up to this command.

234
00:15:03,360 --> 00:15:07,880
And again, before we become self-righteous and judge this guy for asking a crazy question,

235
00:15:07,880 --> 00:15:13,840
I think we likely wrestle with the very same question ourselves today, even if we wouldn't say it out loud.

236
00:15:13,880 --> 00:15:15,200
And maybe that's his mistake.

237
00:15:15,200 --> 00:15:22,280
I regularly see Christians in America redefining the word neighbor or love

238
00:15:22,280 --> 00:15:27,140
so that we can continue to think the way that we think and live the way that we live.

239
00:15:27,140 --> 00:15:35,060
We regularly seek to redefine terms so that we don't actually have to think about obeying Jesus's words.

240
00:15:35,060 --> 00:15:41,520
We can obey parts of Jesus's words and justify away the other words by redefining what the words mean.

241
00:15:42,820 --> 00:15:47,640
My question for us is, what if the ideas we're using from Scripture to justify ourselves,

242
00:15:47,640 --> 00:15:53,400
what if they're watered down and curated to promote our own comfort rather than Christ's kingdom?

243
00:15:53,400 --> 00:15:59,760
Would we be willing to let Jesus confront and challenge us in our place of comfort

244
00:15:59,760 --> 00:16:02,740
into a place of self-giving obedience?

245
00:16:02,740 --> 00:16:08,860
Are we willing to consider the reality that maybe we've built a theology

246
00:16:08,860 --> 00:16:13,940
that allows us to cleanly and neatly obey parts of Jesus's teaching

247
00:16:13,940 --> 00:16:15,860
while explaining away the rest of his teaching?

248
00:16:15,860 --> 00:16:18,680
Because I think that's what this lawyer is trying to do.

249
00:16:18,680 --> 00:16:22,460
I think he's trying to create a theological structure,

250
00:16:22,460 --> 00:16:25,500
create a belief about God, an interpretation to the Scriptures

251
00:16:25,500 --> 00:16:29,800
that allows him to continue to live a comfortable life of partial obedience.

252
00:16:34,800 --> 00:16:38,040
Does that sound like the church in America to anybody else but me?

253
00:16:38,040 --> 00:16:40,820
Okay.

254
00:16:40,820 --> 00:16:42,000
We'll get there.

255
00:16:42,000 --> 00:16:43,300
I'm just warming up, guys.

256
00:16:43,300 --> 00:16:44,220
We're just warming up.

257
00:16:44,220 --> 00:16:44,940
Stick with me.

258
00:16:44,940 --> 00:16:48,260
This is exactly what Jesus does.

259
00:16:48,260 --> 00:16:49,760
Again, he goes through the back door.

260
00:16:49,760 --> 00:16:52,440
He challenges this guy's preconceived ideas

261
00:16:52,440 --> 00:16:56,080
by telling an incredibly beautiful parable

262
00:16:56,080 --> 00:17:00,340
that promotes the surprising ethic of God's upside-down kingdom.

263
00:17:00,340 --> 00:17:03,640
Let's read it, and let's read it slowly and thoughtfully.

264
00:17:03,640 --> 00:17:06,480
Remember, the parables were not intended for somebody

265
00:17:06,480 --> 00:17:09,280
to immediately get up and start explaining what they mean.

266
00:17:09,280 --> 00:17:12,140
Let's just allow the words of Jesus

267
00:17:12,140 --> 00:17:14,820
to confront our thoughts and our emotions.

268
00:17:17,820 --> 00:17:19,520
In reply, Jesus said,

269
00:17:19,520 --> 00:17:22,560
a man was going down from Jerusalem to Jericho

270
00:17:22,560 --> 00:17:25,140
when he was attacked by robbers.

271
00:17:25,140 --> 00:17:28,380
They stripped him of his clothes, they beat him,

272
00:17:28,380 --> 00:17:31,600
and they went away, leaving him half dead.

273
00:17:31,600 --> 00:17:35,980
A priest just happened to be coming down the same road,

274
00:17:35,980 --> 00:17:39,740
and when he saw the man, he passed by on the other side.

275
00:17:40,740 --> 00:17:44,100
So, too, a Levite, when he came to the place

276
00:17:44,100 --> 00:17:47,260
and saw him, passed by on the other side.

277
00:17:47,260 --> 00:17:53,340
But a Samaritan, as he traveled, came where the man was,

278
00:17:53,340 --> 00:17:56,720
and when he saw him, he took pity on him.

279
00:17:56,720 --> 00:17:59,800
He went to him, and he bandaged his wounds,

280
00:17:59,800 --> 00:18:01,820
pouring oil and wine,

281
00:18:01,820 --> 00:18:04,260
and then he put the man on his own donkey

282
00:18:04,260 --> 00:18:06,620
and brought him to an inn to take care of him.

283
00:18:06,620 --> 00:18:09,040
The next day, he took out two denarii

284
00:18:09,040 --> 00:18:10,340
and gave them to the innkeeper.

285
00:18:10,340 --> 00:18:11,720
Look after him, he said,

286
00:18:11,720 --> 00:18:14,540
and when I return, I will reimburse you

287
00:18:14,540 --> 00:18:17,920
for any extra expense you may have.

288
00:18:17,920 --> 00:18:26,160
The more I have spent time studying the parables

289
00:18:26,160 --> 00:18:27,000
the last few weeks,

290
00:18:27,000 --> 00:18:29,800
the more I realize how rich in depth they are,

291
00:18:29,800 --> 00:18:33,600
how there's so much theological implications

292
00:18:33,600 --> 00:18:34,420
in each parable,

293
00:18:34,420 --> 00:18:36,820
whether they're two verses long or seven or 10

294
00:18:36,820 --> 00:18:37,760
or whatever it might be,

295
00:18:37,760 --> 00:18:39,080
there's so much going on.

296
00:18:39,080 --> 00:18:40,780
I heard one pastor explain it this way.

297
00:18:40,780 --> 00:18:42,320
He says that the parables are like

298
00:18:42,320 --> 00:18:43,760
if you put on VR goggles

299
00:18:43,760 --> 00:18:47,880
and you had a virtual reality tour of a house,

300
00:18:47,880 --> 00:18:51,000
it's like plopping yourself in the middle of a parable.

301
00:18:51,000 --> 00:18:52,000
You can just walk around

302
00:18:52,000 --> 00:18:53,680
and you can pick up things in the house

303
00:18:53,680 --> 00:18:55,280
and you can investigate them,

304
00:18:55,280 --> 00:18:56,100
you can examine them.

305
00:18:56,100 --> 00:18:58,160
That's often what interpreting the parables is like.

306
00:18:58,160 --> 00:18:59,700
You get plopped into this story

307
00:18:59,700 --> 00:19:01,920
and there's so much cultural context.

308
00:19:01,920 --> 00:19:02,860
There's so many characters.

309
00:19:02,860 --> 00:19:04,380
There's so many things we could pick up

310
00:19:04,380 --> 00:19:05,560
and look and explore

311
00:19:05,560 --> 00:19:07,200
and they almost all have

312
00:19:07,200 --> 00:19:10,140
some theological implications for our lives.

313
00:19:10,140 --> 00:19:11,160
And in this parable,

314
00:19:11,160 --> 00:19:12,640
there's so much going on.

315
00:19:12,640 --> 00:19:14,260
There's so many things that we could pick out.

316
00:19:14,260 --> 00:19:17,460
I had to leave like 70% of the things

317
00:19:17,460 --> 00:19:19,060
that I found interesting in my study

318
00:19:19,060 --> 00:19:20,140
on the cutting room floor,

319
00:19:20,140 --> 00:19:21,760
which is unfortunate,

320
00:19:21,760 --> 00:19:22,620
but it's okay

321
00:19:22,620 --> 00:19:24,200
because we trust the Holy Spirit

322
00:19:24,200 --> 00:19:24,960
to give us the thing

323
00:19:24,960 --> 00:19:26,260
that we need to hear today, correct?

324
00:19:26,260 --> 00:19:29,120
So what I wanna do is,

325
00:19:29,120 --> 00:19:30,580
I think there's two layers here.

326
00:19:30,640 --> 00:19:33,440
There's two rooms that I wanna investigate with

327
00:19:33,440 --> 00:19:34,780
through the parable.

328
00:19:34,780 --> 00:19:36,520
And I think that they,

329
00:19:36,520 --> 00:19:38,000
if we allow them to,

330
00:19:38,000 --> 00:19:40,180
they will challenge us at first,

331
00:19:40,180 --> 00:19:42,680
convict us,

332
00:19:42,680 --> 00:19:46,760
make us see the ways in which we fall short

333
00:19:46,760 --> 00:19:49,560
on such a regular and consistent basis.

334
00:19:49,560 --> 00:19:52,080
And then at the end,

335
00:19:52,080 --> 00:19:53,320
I think we'll be encouraged

336
00:19:53,320 --> 00:19:54,760
and feel loved by God

337
00:19:54,760 --> 00:19:55,580
who forgives us

338
00:19:55,580 --> 00:19:57,100
even in the midst of our failure.

339
00:19:57,100 --> 00:19:59,600
That's the plan for the rest of the time.

340
00:19:59,640 --> 00:20:00,520
Does that sound okay?

341
00:20:00,520 --> 00:20:01,760
All right,

342
00:20:01,760 --> 00:20:03,740
so the first layer of this parable

343
00:20:03,740 --> 00:20:04,680
that I wanna look at

344
00:20:04,680 --> 00:20:07,440
is the most obvious intended implication

345
00:20:07,440 --> 00:20:08,240
for the lawyer,

346
00:20:08,240 --> 00:20:10,720
for the expert of the law,

347
00:20:10,720 --> 00:20:11,380
and subsequently,

348
00:20:11,380 --> 00:20:13,100
anyone seeking to understand

349
00:20:13,100 --> 00:20:14,220
and apply the command

350
00:20:14,220 --> 00:20:15,560
to love the Lord your God

351
00:20:15,560 --> 00:20:17,100
with all of your heart, soul, mind, and strength

352
00:20:17,100 --> 00:20:19,080
and to love your neighbor as yourself.

353
00:20:19,080 --> 00:20:20,680
Jesus is telling this story

354
00:20:20,680 --> 00:20:21,980
to answer his question,

355
00:20:21,980 --> 00:20:22,880
who is your neighbor?

356
00:20:22,880 --> 00:20:24,600
How do I love God

357
00:20:24,600 --> 00:20:25,620
and love my neighbor fully?

358
00:20:25,620 --> 00:20:27,020
In 2,000 years removed

359
00:20:27,020 --> 00:20:28,220
from this cultural moment,

360
00:20:28,900 --> 00:20:30,800
it seems like a pretty cut and dry story

361
00:20:30,800 --> 00:20:33,000
about some self-righteous religious jerks

362
00:20:33,000 --> 00:20:33,900
getting God wrong

363
00:20:33,900 --> 00:20:35,900
and a religious outsider

364
00:20:35,900 --> 00:20:37,400
getting God right.

365
00:20:37,400 --> 00:20:38,860
Does that sound

366
00:20:38,860 --> 00:20:42,100
like a decent cursory reading of the text?

367
00:20:43,060 --> 00:20:44,860
But I think this distant approach,

368
00:20:44,860 --> 00:20:45,200
again,

369
00:20:45,200 --> 00:20:46,680
allows us to keep from seeing

370
00:20:46,680 --> 00:20:48,020
how the attitudes of the priest

371
00:20:48,020 --> 00:20:48,600
and the Levite

372
00:20:48,600 --> 00:20:50,340
might still be very alive

373
00:20:50,340 --> 00:20:51,980
in our hearts today.

374
00:20:53,280 --> 00:20:55,240
We have to humanize these characters.

375
00:20:55,240 --> 00:20:56,240
We have to realize

376
00:20:56,240 --> 00:20:57,840
the context that they were in,

377
00:20:57,840 --> 00:20:59,880
the tensions that they were dealing with,

378
00:20:59,880 --> 00:21:01,020
because all of us,

379
00:21:01,020 --> 00:21:03,660
all of our lives are complicated

380
00:21:03,660 --> 00:21:04,420
and nuanced,

381
00:21:04,420 --> 00:21:05,320
and none of us

382
00:21:05,320 --> 00:21:08,680
have such simplicity

383
00:21:08,680 --> 00:21:10,660
that we like to read in the text.

384
00:21:10,660 --> 00:21:11,100
And so,

385
00:21:11,100 --> 00:21:12,160
I want to think about this.

386
00:21:12,160 --> 00:21:13,200
The Levite and the priest,

387
00:21:13,200 --> 00:21:14,560
they had a significant role

388
00:21:14,560 --> 00:21:15,960
in the life of the nation of Israel

389
00:21:15,960 --> 00:21:17,560
during a very tumultuous time.

390
00:21:18,300 --> 00:21:19,380
These men were tasked

391
00:21:19,380 --> 00:21:20,100
with preserving

392
00:21:20,100 --> 00:21:21,300
the very identity

393
00:21:21,300 --> 00:21:22,920
of their nation

394
00:21:22,920 --> 00:21:24,920
in the midst of tremendous pressure

395
00:21:24,920 --> 00:21:25,720
both inside

396
00:21:25,720 --> 00:21:26,880
and outside their ranks.

397
00:21:26,880 --> 00:21:27,560
There was corruption

398
00:21:27,560 --> 00:21:29,260
in the Jewish world.

399
00:21:29,260 --> 00:21:29,900
There was corruption

400
00:21:29,900 --> 00:21:30,940
outside the Jewish world.

401
00:21:30,940 --> 00:21:31,760
There was persecution

402
00:21:31,760 --> 00:21:34,300
of the people of Israel.

403
00:21:34,300 --> 00:21:35,820
And it was their job

404
00:21:35,820 --> 00:21:36,780
to hold the line,

405
00:21:36,780 --> 00:21:38,160
to lead by example

406
00:21:38,160 --> 00:21:39,820
on obedience to God

407
00:21:39,820 --> 00:21:41,320
and proper worship of Him

408
00:21:41,320 --> 00:21:42,600
in a time when compromise

409
00:21:42,600 --> 00:21:43,880
and rebellion was rampant.

410
00:21:43,880 --> 00:21:45,920
I'm not saying

411
00:21:45,920 --> 00:21:46,820
they did the right thing

412
00:21:46,820 --> 00:21:47,800
because they didn't.

413
00:21:48,300 --> 00:21:49,120
But if we ignore

414
00:21:49,120 --> 00:21:50,300
the complex factors

415
00:21:50,300 --> 00:21:51,380
involved in their decision,

416
00:21:51,380 --> 00:21:52,580
we might miss the ways

417
00:21:52,580 --> 00:21:54,460
that we make similar decisions today.

418
00:21:54,460 --> 00:21:58,540
I think Christians in America,

419
00:21:58,540 --> 00:22:00,760
we have this pressure

420
00:22:00,760 --> 00:22:03,680
with outside forces coming in

421
00:22:03,680 --> 00:22:05,160
and with inside forces

422
00:22:05,160 --> 00:22:06,520
redefining things

423
00:22:06,520 --> 00:22:07,340
that we have held true

424
00:22:07,340 --> 00:22:08,180
for 2,000 years.

425
00:22:08,180 --> 00:22:09,060
We feel this pressure

426
00:22:09,060 --> 00:22:10,840
to hold the line on truth,

427
00:22:10,840 --> 00:22:12,020
to hold the line

428
00:22:12,020 --> 00:22:13,060
on obedience to God

429
00:22:13,060 --> 00:22:14,200
and worship of God.

430
00:22:14,200 --> 00:22:15,500
And I think sometimes

431
00:22:15,500 --> 00:22:17,220
I see this in our day

432
00:22:17,220 --> 00:22:17,840
and age today

433
00:22:17,840 --> 00:22:18,600
where sometimes

434
00:22:18,600 --> 00:22:19,980
we feel this pressure

435
00:22:19,980 --> 00:22:20,700
and we're trying

436
00:22:20,700 --> 00:22:22,800
to hold Christianity together.

437
00:22:22,800 --> 00:22:23,720
We're trying to be obedient.

438
00:22:23,720 --> 00:22:24,760
We're trying to be faithful.

439
00:22:24,760 --> 00:22:28,580
And we're so focused on that

440
00:22:28,580 --> 00:22:29,920
that we miss obedience

441
00:22:29,920 --> 00:22:31,240
to God all around us.

442
00:22:31,240 --> 00:22:33,640
And I think it would be easy

443
00:22:33,640 --> 00:22:34,400
for an outsider

444
00:22:34,400 --> 00:22:35,260
to look in and say,

445
00:22:35,260 --> 00:22:37,260
oh, you Christians,

446
00:22:37,260 --> 00:22:38,400
like you're really good

447
00:22:38,400 --> 00:22:40,360
at saying the right things,

448
00:22:40,360 --> 00:22:41,840
at putting the right things

449
00:22:41,840 --> 00:22:42,300
on paper,

450
00:22:42,300 --> 00:22:43,680
but what are you doing

451
00:22:43,680 --> 00:22:45,200
with the people around you

452
00:22:45,200 --> 00:22:45,840
who are suffering?

453
00:22:45,840 --> 00:22:49,420
So I don't think it's,

454
00:22:49,420 --> 00:22:50,780
I don't think we can get away

455
00:22:50,780 --> 00:22:52,700
with just like demonizing

456
00:22:52,700 --> 00:22:53,360
these characters

457
00:22:53,360 --> 00:22:54,880
and justifying it away.

458
00:22:54,880 --> 00:22:56,540
I think they had three

459
00:22:56,540 --> 00:22:57,380
primary tensions

460
00:22:57,380 --> 00:22:58,340
that they were wrestling with.

461
00:22:58,340 --> 00:22:59,600
The first is fear of safety.

462
00:22:59,600 --> 00:23:01,540
It's a very dangerous road.

463
00:23:01,540 --> 00:23:02,540
It's very possible

464
00:23:02,540 --> 00:23:03,280
that the robbers

465
00:23:03,280 --> 00:23:03,960
who beat this man

466
00:23:03,960 --> 00:23:04,620
half to death

467
00:23:04,620 --> 00:23:06,780
were using this half dead man

468
00:23:06,780 --> 00:23:07,300
as a trap

469
00:23:07,300 --> 00:23:08,860
to get some compassionate person

470
00:23:08,860 --> 00:23:09,380
to come in

471
00:23:09,380 --> 00:23:10,320
so that they could beat him

472
00:23:10,320 --> 00:23:10,880
half to death

473
00:23:10,880 --> 00:23:11,940
and take all of his stuff,

474
00:23:11,940 --> 00:23:12,700
right?

475
00:23:12,700 --> 00:23:14,060
There's fear of safety

476
00:23:14,060 --> 00:23:15,560
and security in this text.

477
00:23:15,560 --> 00:23:16,860
It could be easy

478
00:23:16,860 --> 00:23:17,360
for somebody

479
00:23:17,360 --> 00:23:18,500
who feels self-important,

480
00:23:18,500 --> 00:23:20,220
who feels like their role

481
00:23:20,220 --> 00:23:21,400
as a priest or a Levite

482
00:23:21,400 --> 00:23:22,900
serving God in the temple,

483
00:23:22,900 --> 00:23:24,500
they can't take the risk

484
00:23:24,500 --> 00:23:26,120
of getting hurt

485
00:23:26,120 --> 00:23:27,100
because if they get hurt,

486
00:23:27,100 --> 00:23:28,300
then the temple's

487
00:23:28,300 --> 00:23:29,440
gonna be short-staffed,

488
00:23:29,440 --> 00:23:29,700
right?

489
00:23:29,700 --> 00:23:31,700
The worship of God

490
00:23:31,700 --> 00:23:33,860
could go by the wayside.

491
00:23:33,860 --> 00:23:35,140
I've gotta make sure I'm safe.

492
00:23:35,140 --> 00:23:36,740
There's purity laws

493
00:23:36,740 --> 00:23:37,480
in the Old Testament

494
00:23:37,480 --> 00:23:38,000
that would have

495
00:23:38,000 --> 00:23:40,200
made it very embarrassing

496
00:23:40,200 --> 00:23:40,960
and costly

497
00:23:40,960 --> 00:23:42,300
and inconvenient for them

498
00:23:42,300 --> 00:23:42,840
if they happened

499
00:23:42,840 --> 00:23:44,220
to touch a dead person.

500
00:23:44,220 --> 00:23:45,140
So they don't even

501
00:23:45,140 --> 00:23:45,940
get close enough

502
00:23:45,940 --> 00:23:46,840
to know whether or not

503
00:23:46,840 --> 00:23:47,440
he's dead or not.

504
00:23:47,440 --> 00:23:48,480
He looks dead,

505
00:23:48,480 --> 00:23:49,500
they assume he's dead

506
00:23:49,500 --> 00:23:50,980
and they're not going

507
00:23:50,980 --> 00:23:51,880
to take on

508
00:23:51,880 --> 00:23:54,080
the difficulty

509
00:23:54,080 --> 00:23:55,600
of dealing with this person

510
00:23:55,600 --> 00:23:56,400
even though

511
00:23:56,400 --> 00:23:58,540
in many Jewish teachings

512
00:23:58,540 --> 00:23:59,080
of the day

513
00:23:59,080 --> 00:24:00,420
taking care of a dead person

514
00:24:00,420 --> 00:24:01,000
was like something

515
00:24:01,000 --> 00:24:01,840
that they absolutely

516
00:24:01,840 --> 00:24:02,820
should have done.

517
00:24:02,820 --> 00:24:04,860
